<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    FS.GG.Kit receiver (ADR-0062, .github#1262 / .github#1291). This repo is a `coordination-kit`
    receiver: it holds the shared coordination skills, the `fsgg-coord` client, and the engine tool
    manifest on disk. Net was rostered for the kit at creation but never wired for it (.github#1291);
    it is onboarded DIRECTLY onto package delivery — it REFERENCES the versioned `FS.GG.Kit` package
    and MATERIALIZES that kit onto disk from its pin, skipping the byte-copy step entirely. A hub
    change reaches this repo as an ordinary Renovate bump of the pin (in Directory.Packages.props), and
    `kit-materialize.yml` re-runs the materialize on that PR — the same shape lockfile-sync uses for
    packages.lock.json.

    This project exists ONLY to reference the package and drive its materialize. It compiles nothing and
    is deliberately NOT in FS.GG.Net.slnx, so the normal build never touches it. Materialize explicitly:

        dotnet build .config/kit/FS.GG.Kit.receiver.proj -t:FsggKitMaterialize

    The materialized files (skills, scripts/fsgg-coord, .config/dotnet-tools.json) are COMMITTED: the
    materialize is the write arm that replaces the byte-copy, not a live per-build input, so it does NOT
    run on every build (FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild=false, per the .github#1280 review). The committed bytes
    are content-addressed and byte-identical to canonical, so `coordination-coherence` stays green.

    This repo does NOT `receive: build-config` (it hand-authors its own Directory.Build.props /
    Directory.Packages.props, like audio/templates), so FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig stays off (its
    default) — the kit materialize never touches those files.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A TFM is required by the SDK; nothing is compiled. Matches the repo's band (global.json). -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Tooling-only: no source, no output, no lockfile of its own (the gate locks the solution, not this). -->
    <EnableDefaultItems>false</EnableDefaultItems>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>false</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

    <!-- ADR-0067 §9 phase 4 (FS-GG/.github#1721). `.agents/skills` is a VIEW root now, not a
         materialized one: still in the runtime contract, still discovered by Codex with no
         configuration, but its content is RESOLVED from `.claude/skills` at checkout by
         `scripts/skill-view generate` instead of being a second committed copy of it. 23 committed
         files stopped existing; nothing was retired.

         THE RUNTIME ROOT SET IS UNCHANGED. ADR-0065 §A root's three dispositions makes it the UNION
         of these two properties, and that union is still ADR-0011's two roots as amended by ADR-0067
         §5. Moving a root between the two properties is a disposition change, not a contract change.

         WHY `.claude/skills` IS THE LIVE ROOT AND NOT THE VIEW. The kit's four skill rows source
         `.claude/skills/<id>` in FS-GG/.github `registry/repos.yml`, and `registry/repos.lock`
         content-addresses those exact paths — so the materialize writes there, and
         `coordination-coherence` grades those bytes against this repo's pin. Making `.agents/skills`
         the live root instead would rename a `kit:` source, which obliges a kit republish and a
         seven-receiver fan-out for a path rename. See FS-GG/.github
         `docs/coordination/skill-apparatus-retirement-order.md` §3. -->
    <FsggKitSkillRoots>.claude/skills</FsggKitSkillRoots>
    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>.agents/skills</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>

    <!-- Materialize into the repo ROOT (two levels up from .config/kit/), on demand — never on build. -->
    <FsggKitReceiverRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../'))</FsggKitReceiverRoot>
    <FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>false</FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Version pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.props (CPM; Renovate-managed), like every other pin. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FS.GG.Kit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE VIEW IS GENERATED BEFORE IT IS ASSERTED, IN EVERY TREE THE MATERIALIZE RUNS IN.

    `FsggKitCheckSkillView` (ADR-0067 §8, AfterTargets=FsggKitMaterialize) fails the build when a view
    root is absent, dangling, or partly visible — deliberately, because all three of those are exit 0
    with no diagnostic in BOTH runtimes, and "a rewrite that removes the loud failure and adds the
    quiet one is worse than no rewrite." A view root is NOT committed (a committed symlink degrades to
    a plain text file under `git -c core.symlinks=false`, ADR-0067 §6), so EVERY fresh checkout starts
    without one: a clean clone, a new worktree, and `kit-materialize.yml`'s runner alike. Measured on
    this repo at the retirement commit — without this target the materialize FAILS with
    "view skill root '.agents/skills' is ABSENT or a DANGLING link".

    So the generate belongs next to the assertion, not in a workflow step that only some of those
    trees run. The tool is `scripts/skill-view` — THIS repo's copy, delivered and byte-verified by the
    FS.GG.Kit version this repo pins, never one fetched from a moving hub `main`: a receiver's verdict
    must be a function of the receiver (FS-GG/.github#1584).

    The `roots` flag names the view roots EXPLICITLY rather than letting the tool fall back to
    ADR-0011's default pair. The fallback would also try to resolve `.claude/skills`, this repo's
    TRACKED live root, and `skill-view` refuses to generate over a tracked root with no override, so
    it would be a refusal rather than a no-op.

    NOT conditioned on the OS. `skill-view` is bash; there is no Windows CI here, and a Windows tree
    without bash SHOULD fail loudly rather than quietly materialize a kit whose second runtime root is
    empty. The `Condition` guards on the PROPERTY, so if a later change empties FsggKitViewSkillRoots
    this target stops running with it instead of generating a view nothing asserts.
  -->
  <Target Name="FsggNetGenerateSkillView" BeforeTargets="FsggKitCheckSkillView"
          Condition="'$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots)' != ''">
    <Exec WorkingDirectory="$(FsggKitReceiverRoot)"
          Command="bash scripts/skill-view generate --source $(FsggKitSkillRoots) --roots &quot;$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots.Replace(';',' '))&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
